Original commit body from f2c25a5e09612254af160ffa67570bc53ad51c9b:

An audit of the ADRs and docs/hypergumbo-spec.md found the decisions sound
— no two ADRs rule opposite ways — but six defects in the status-and-count
layer, one of them structural.

generate-architecture skipped every underscore-prefixed filename. Correct
for private helpers (_concept_utils, _text_filters, _view_template_core);
wrong for linkers/_third_party_bases.py, which is underscore-named AND
registers django-third-party-dispatch-linker. The generated count read 60
against a registry of 61, and the spec, LINKERS.md, ARCHITECTURE.md and
ADR-3bbb's Status line all inherited it. Worse, ADR-3bbb's enforcement
promise — "the Uncategorized counter is 0 and will surface any future
regression" — had a hole exactly the shape of this file: a module the scan
skips can be flagged neither categorized nor Uncategorized. The scan now
decides on the presence of @register_linker rather than on the filename.

The rest:

- LINKERS.md was not the full catalogue the spec sends readers to it for.
  Four registered linkers had no row (argparse_dispatch,
  caddy_module_dispatch, receiver_type_dispatch, _third_party_bases); three
  appeared nowhere in the file. Now 61 rows against a registry of 61.
- Spec §12 claimed emit_module_attribute_refs is "wired into six analyzers
  — c, cpp, go, java, js_ts, rust". c.py makes zero calls and zero imports
  of it; its only mention is a comment explaining the helper cannot match
  bare-identifier stdio globals, which is why C carries its own
  _C_STDIO_GLOBALS table. Five, plus C by a separate mechanism. The edges
  are real either way — only the account of how was false.
- taxonomy.py covers 89 languages, not 86.
- §3 said the I/O catalogue supports 16 languages (14 + 2 aliases) while
  §12 said 15 catalogues. Disk says 15 + 2 = 17; §12 was right.
- Four supersession records were one-sided, against the README's rule that
  both endpoints name each other by number. ADR-0034 declared itself
  partially superseded by ADR-0036 while ADR-0036's Supersedes read "—"
  and buried the relationship in a parenthetical — a contradiction between
  two files, not a formatting nit. Plus ADR-0024/0038 and both ADR-3aaa
  extensions.

Two gates close the classes rather than the instances: every module that
registers a linker must be inventoried as one, and supersession must be
symmetric. The symmetry parser strips parenthetical citations before
reading ADR numbers, because a Supersedes line routinely names an
unrelated ADR to say why something is dead (ADR-0035 cites ADR-0032 that
way) — reading the raw line reports that citation as a claim, which is the
false positive that motivated the strip.

Disclosed, not worked around: the symmetry test is registered in
KNOWN_UNREACHABLE because its subject is markdown. smart-test writes a
zero-test manifest when only docs changed, so a docs-only ADR edit — the
exact PR shape this guards — runs no tests per-PR and the asymmetry lands
in full-suite instead. Closing that means teaching the mapper to claim
docs/adr/**, and the mapper is a governance file.

No package code changed, so no version bump.

